---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const shrank = assertion('the-serious-court-shrank-and-the-everyday-one-grew')
const crime = assertion('crime-is-the-part-that-did-not-move')
const juv = assertion('juvenile-filings-fell-by-two-fifths')
const judges = assertion('seven-judges-hear-all-of-it')
const coll = assertion('one-collapsed-and-the-other-did-not-move')
const three = assertion('three-renters-for-every-owner')
const match = assertion('the-same-numbers-from-two-covers')

const spec = (a: typeof shrank, yTitle: string) =>
  bar(
    a.figures.map((f) => f.label),
    a.figures.map((f) => f.value),
    { yTitle, labelValues: true },
  )

const shrankSpec = spec(shrank, 'New cases filed in a year')
const crimeSpec = spec(crime, 'New cases filed in a year')
const collSpec = spec(coll, 'Filings in a year')
const threeSpec = spec(three, 'Filings in a year')
---

<Article slug="what-comes-to-the-courthouse">
  <p>
    Ohio's courts report themselves to Columbus every year, case type by case type, and until 2017
    the Supreme Court published the whole thing county by county in a document of nearly three
    hundred pages. Eleven of those reports cover Allen County, which means eleven years of what
    actually arrived at its courthouses rather than what anybody remembers arriving.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={shrank} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="shrank"
    title="Two courts, eleven years apart"
    caption="New filings in the county's court of common pleas, all four divisions, against the Lima Municipal Court's."
    columns={['Court and year', 'Filings']}
    rows={shrank.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="shrank" set:html={JSON.stringify(shrankSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    The municipal court is the one most people meet. It serves the whole county rather than the
    city — its population column is the county's census count — and twenty-one cases arrive there
    for every one that reaches the county's civil trial court. Its own filings swing thirty-eight
    per cent in five years, which is what a docket does when charging or collection practice changes
    and nothing outside the courthouse changes at all.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={crime} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="crime"
    title="Criminal and civil, at the two ends of the run"
    caption="General division of the court of common pleas. The criminal count runs 400 to 537 across the eleven years without a trend in it."
    columns={['Series and year', 'Filings']}
    rows={crime.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="crime" set:html={JSON.stringify(crimeSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    So the fall in this county's most serious court is entirely a fall in civil business. Felonies
    kept arriving at the same rate for eleven years; the lawsuits stopped.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={juv} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={judges} />
  <p>
    The bench did not shrink with the docket. Seven judges heard everything in 2007 and seven heard
    everything in 2017, so the fall shows up as work per judge rather than as fewer judges: 953 new
    cases each in the general division at the start of the run and 588 at the end. Juvenile is the
    division that emptied fastest, and a county with fewer children in it is the first thing to
    suspect — though a filing is a decision by a prosecutor or an agency before it is a fact about a
    child, and the report cannot separate the two.
  </p>

  <h2>The two ways a home is lost</h2>
  <p>
    Both of them run through this county's courts, and they run through different ones. A
    foreclosure is a civil action in the general division of the common pleas court; an eviction is a
    forcible entry and detainer complaint at the municipal court. Between 2007 and 2017 the county
    saw 5,534 of the first and 9,622 of the second.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={coll} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="coll"
    title="One series moves and one does not"
    caption="Foreclosure filings at their 2008 peak and in 2017, against the eleven-year average for evictions. The eviction series has a standard deviation of 55 around that mean."
    columns={['Measure', 'Filings']}
    rows={coll.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="coll" set:html={JSON.stringify(collSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={three} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="three"
    title="Owners and renters, 2008 and 2016"
    caption="Foreclosure and eviction filings in the county's two courts. The composition changes entirely and the total does not."
    columns={['Series and year', 'Filings']}
    rows={three.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="three" set:html={JSON.stringify(threeSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    The housing crisis is enormous in one of these series and invisible in the other. Whatever kept
    eight or nine hundred Allen County households a year in front of a judge over rent was not
    working through the same machinery that put owners there, and it did not stop when that
    machinery did. The 2008 pairing is the one worth sitting with: in that year this county's courts
    took 996 foreclosure filings and 982 eviction filings, almost exactly one apiece, and by 2016
    the same courts took 279 and 913.
  </p>

  <h2>The same numbers from two covers</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={match} />
  <Gloss>
    Which looks like corroboration and is not. A research lab in New Jersey and the judicial branch
    of a state government agreeing to the unit on a count near a thousand would be very strong
    evidence, if they were counting separately. They are not: both chains begin at the Lima
    Municipal Court's own docket, reported to Columbus under one rule and obtained from the court by
    the lab under another. The exactness is the tell — two independent counts of a real quantity
    nearly always differ a little, because they draw their boundaries in slightly different places.
    What the agreement establishes is that neither pipeline dropped or misaligned anything between
    the registry and the page, which is worth knowing and is not the same thing.
  </Gloss>
  <p>
    The four-case gap in 2017 is the informative part. It shows the two are not literally the same
    file, and it is the only available measurement of how far apart these pipelines can drift.
  </p>
</Article>
